<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Algeria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Algeria is a country in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. Its territory formed part of medieval empires such as the Almoravid domains and was later an Ottoman province governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> conquered it from 1830, making it the most important settler colony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>, until independence in 1962.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/algeria.jpg" alt="Algeria" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: National Liberation Front of Algeria Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Algeria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Algeria is the largest country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> basin, covering 2,381,741 square kilometres. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> covers most of it. In the north, the Tell Atlas and the Saharan Atlas form two parallel sets of reliefs, and the area from the coast to the Tell Atlas is fertile; south of it lies steppe, then the desert. The highest point is Mount Tahat, at 3,003 metres, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaggar-mountains">Hoggar Mountains</a> of the central Sahara. About 90% of Algerians live in the northern coastal area, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a>, Constantine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annaba">Annaba</a> are the main cities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Early finds show that hominins lived on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> fringe of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> earlier than previously thought. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> life, richly depicted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tassili-najjer">Tassili n'Ajjer</a> paintings, predominated until the classical period. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> established settlements on the coast, and by 600 BCE a Phoenician presence existed at places such as Icosium, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a>.</p><p>They avoided conquering the strong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes of the interior, collected tribute and traded, and the Berbers supplied them with goods, slaves and skilled cavalry. Amazigh societies, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numidians">Numidians</a>, whose kingdom became one of the earliest major states in the region, were also shaped by Roman, Vandal and Byzantine influences.</p><p>From the 7th century the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the arrival of Arab tribes gradually transformed the region, and from the 8th to the 15th centuries local Islamic dynasties ruled. The Regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, established in 1516, became a largely autonomous state owing nominal allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. France invaded in 1830 and formally annexed Algeria in 1848, though it was not fully conquered until 1903. French rule brought mass European settlement that displaced the local population, and by the mid-1870s indigenous Algerians had declined by up to a third through warfare, disease and starvation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Resistance to French colonisation ran from the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir-abdelkader">Emir Abdelkader</a> and the Mokrani Revolt to the nationalist movements of the 20th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setif-and-guelma-massacre">Sétif and Guelma massacre</a> in 1945 catalysed the resistance that led to the outbreak of the Algerian War in 1954, and Algeria gained independence in 1962. A civil war lasted from 1992 to 2002. The country is a semi-presidential republic with a population of about 47 million. Its official languages are Arabic and Amazigh, most people speak Algerian Arabic, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> is the official religion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Algeria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ibrahim's charge covered what is now eastern <b>Algeria</b>, Tunisia and Tripolitania, and in theory the whole Maghreb west of Cyrenaica, since the territory granted him was never demarcated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Almoravid economy rested on three things: control of the trans-Saharan gold trade, the agricultural revenues of Morocco and western <b>Algeria</b>, and the urban economy of al-Andalus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>A member of the Eastern Bloc, North Vietnam was recognised by almost all communist countries, China first, and received their aid; many non-aligned states gave it de facto recognition, and its clandestine weapon transfers during the Algerian War made relations with <b>Algeria</b> close.</blockquote><a href="/empire/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Franco seriously considered joining for the sake of ideology and of claims to Gibraltar, French Morocco and the Oran district of <b>Algeria</b>, and took over the international city of Tangier after the defeat of France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/francoist-spain">Francoist Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The French Foreign Legion was founded in 1831 for service in <b>Algeria</b> and kept its headquarters at Sidi Bel Abbès until Algerian independence in 1962.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Algeria</b>, organised into French departments from 1848, attracted around one million European settlers, the pieds-noirs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
